(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man becomes ill over 4 days with a temperature of 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills and confusion. He looks toxic, a new loud regurgitant murmur is heard, and painless hemorrhagic macules appear on his soles, which are Janeway lesions (painless, nontender spots). Which form of infective endocarditis fits his course?","choices":["Subacute endocarditis","Libman-Sacks disease","Device endocarditis","Acute endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Four days of illness with 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills, confusion and a toxic appearance fit acute endocarditis: a hectically febrile illness from a virulent, invasive organism, with rapid onset typically over 3-10 days. The new loud murmur reflects rapid valve damage, and Janeway lesions show seeding beyond the heart. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman had a mechanical mitral valve placed 4 years ago. She has 5 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and blood cultures are pending. Which organism is most likely in her prosthetic valve infection?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Candida albicans","Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her mechanical valve was placed 4 years ago, so this is late prosthetic valve endocarditis, which is caused by Streptococcus viridans. The 5-week course of low-grade fever and fatigue also fits, since viridans streptococci are a classic subacute organism; early infection would instead be staphylococcal. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his aortic valve replaced 6 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and transesophageal imaging shows a vegetation on the prosthesis. Which organisms are most likely?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococci from urine","Airway gram-negatives","Staphylococci from the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A vegetation on an aortic prosthesis placed only 6 weeks ago is early prosthetic valve endocarditis, which is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci (Staphylococcus epidermidis) and Staphylococcus aureus, both skin organisms. The recent surgery is the decisive clue. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has a fever of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). No blood cultures or echocardiogram have been done yet. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Endocarditis ruled out","No criteria are met","Possible endocarditis","Definite endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He has three minor criteria: a predisposing heart condition (prosthetic aortic valve), fever of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), above 38 °C, and an embolic phenomenon, conjunctival hemorrhage. With no cultures or echocardiogram there is no major criterion, and three minor criteria make endocarditis possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis needs a root canal. She developed hives (urticaria) after amoxicillin years ago and can take pills. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","Oral amoxicillin","Oral clindamycin","Oral azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Previous endocarditis qualifies her, and a root canal manipulates the periapical region of the tooth, so she needs prophylaxis. She can take pills but had urticaria with amoxicillin, which rules out penicillins and cephalexin, leaving azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg (or doxycycline) 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and fatigue. A new murmur with a tumor plop (a low-pitched early diastolic sound) is heard, and three sets of blood cultures drawn before any antibiotics are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Lyme disease","Atrial myxoma","Subacute endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial myxoma mimics endocarditis with fever and a new murmur, classically a tumor plop or mid-diastolic rumble. Her 2 months of fever and weight loss could suggest endocarditis, but the tumor plop and three negative culture sets drawn before any antibiotics point to this neoplastic mimic. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis develops worsening breathlessness, crackles and a new loud regurgitant murmur on day 3 of appropriate antibiotics. Echocardiography shows a ruptured chord with severe regurgitation. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Palliative care alone","Outpatient cardiology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On day 3 of appropriate antibiotics she has breathlessness, crackles, a new loud regurgitant murmur and a ruptured chord with severe regurgitation: heart failure due to valvular dysfunction, the most common indication for surgery. Cardiothoracic surgery is consulted to evaluate the need for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with aortic valve endocarditis develops sharp chest pain and a scratchy friction rub on day 5 of treatment. Imaging shows infection spreading beyond the valve ring. Which process explains the new pericardial findings?","choices":["Abscess extending into the septum","Septic emboli to the coronary arteries","Drug-induced lupus from antibiotics","Abscess extending into the epicardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infection spreading beyond the valve ring is a perivalvular abscess, and when it extends into the epicardium it causes pericarditis, explaining his sharp chest pain and friction rub. Extension into the upper ventricular septum would instead interrupt the conduction system. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve needs a dental extraction. He had a mild delayed rash (no hives, swelling or anaphylaxis) with penicillin and cannot take oral medication. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Clindamycin injection","Cefazolin by injection","Ampicillin injection","Oral azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is penicillin-allergic and cannot take oral medication, so the regimen is cefazolin or ceftriaxone 1 g intravenously or intramuscularly 30-60 minutes before the extraction. His reaction was a mild delayed rash, not anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria, so a cephalosporin is allowed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has had 2 months of low-grade fevers to 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), fatigue and a 4-kilogram weight loss. She had a tooth extracted 10 weeks ago. A new apical systolic murmur is heard, and splinter hemorrhages (thin dark lines under the nail plates) are seen on two fingers. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Acute infective endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two months of lower fevers (38.4 °C), fatigue and a 4-kilogram weight loss is the indolent course of subacute infective endocarditis, in which fever, malaise and weight loss occur over weeks or months. The new murmur and splinter hemorrhages place the problem on a valve, and the recent extraction is a plausible dental source. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. His son asks how serious this organism is. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It carries a good prognosis","It rarely needs any surgery","It resolves within a week","It is the most lethal cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism in infective endocarditis, with mortality of about 30% in people who do not inject drugs, 50% in prosthetic valve endocarditis and 5% in people who inject drugs. His prosthetic aortic valve puts him in the highest-mortality group. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 8-year-old with endocarditis has a sudden fulminant illness with high spiking fevers, unlike the usual slow course in children. Which organisms does this presentation suggest?","choices":["Candida or other fungi","Pneumococcus or Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most children have an indolent course with prolonged low-grade fever, but a fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests Streptococcus pneumoniae or Staphylococcus aureus. The abrupt, high-fever onset in this 8-year-old is the decisive clue. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve placed 3 years ago has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin, rifampin and gentamicin","Nafcillin, rifampin and gentamicin","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Penicillin G plus gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus infection of a prosthetic valve is treated with combination therapy: an antistaphylococcal drug plus rifampin and gentamicin. Because his strain is methicillin-resistant, that drug is vancomycin (nafcillin or oxacillin would be used only for a susceptible strain). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is on day 6 of antibiotics for aortic valve endocarditis. He still has fevers, and today's electrocardiogram shows a new conduction block not present on admission. Which complication best explains the new conduction abnormality?","choices":["Septal perivalvular abscess","Septic emboli to the lungs","Spread into the epicardium","Chordae tendineae rupture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent fever on day 6 with a new conduction block suggests a perivalvular abscess, meaning infection that extends beyond the valve leaflets into myocardial tissue. When the abscess extends into the upper ventricular septum, it interrupts the conduction system. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Which organism is a classic cause of this tempo of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three days of fever to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), rigors, a new murmur and a toxic appearance are an acute tempo, and the acute organisms are beta-hemolytic streptococci, Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible or methicillin-resistant), sometimes Staphylococcus lugdunensis, and enterococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve has a fever of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F). Three blood cultures grow viridans streptococci, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows new partial dehiscence of the prosthesis. How many major Duke criteria does he meet?","choices":["No major criteria","One major criterion","Two major criteria","Three major criteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci, a typical organism, in separate blood cultures meet the major blood culture criterion, and new partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve on transesophageal echocardiogram meets the major endocardial criterion. Two major criteria make endocarditis definite; his fever adds a minor criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.4 °C (102.9 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless hemorrhagic macules) on the soles of both feet. Blood cultures are pending. Under the modified Duke criteria, which category do the Janeway lesions fall into?","choices":["Microbiologic evidence","Vascular phenomenon","Immunologic phenomenon","Major endocardial criterion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions, the painless hemorrhagic macules on his soles, are a vascular phenomenon, a minor criterion grouped with major arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial hemorrhage and conjunctival hemorrhages. His injection drug use and fever of 39.4 °C (102.9 °F) are separate minor criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with native valve endocarditis is improving on day 4 of intravenous antibiotics and asks whether he can switch to pills so he can go home sooner. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Two more days, then stop","Stop when the fever is gone","Therapy stays parenteral","Pills work just as well now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics for endocarditis should be given parenterally, and therapy must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture, because all bacteria in the vegetation must be killed. On day 4 the course has barely begun, so he stays on intravenous therapy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 12-year-old had a ventricular septal defect closed with a prosthetic patch 4 months ago and is now scheduled for a dental extraction. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Delay the extraction a year","Give antibiotics afterward","Give prophylaxis beforehand","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 months after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is a high-risk pediatric condition, and he is only 4 months out from his patch. A dental extraction manipulates gingival and periapical tissue, so a single prophylactic dose is given 30-60 minutes before the procedure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 17-year-old who injects drugs has fever, rigors and a tricuspid valve vegetation. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Candida species","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans group streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adolescents increasingly resemble adults, including injection drug use-associated Staphylococcus aureus disease. His drug use, fever with rigors and a tricuspid valve vegetation fit that adult-type profile rather than the viridans streptococcal disease of younger children. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs has finished a course of intravenous antibiotics for tricuspid valve endocarditis and is preparing for discharge. He says he wants help to stay safe. Which consultation should be arranged?","choices":["Nephrology","Addiction medicine","Dermatology","Ophthalmology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He injects drugs, has just finished treatment for tricuspid valve endocarditis and asks for help to stay safe, which is harm reduction. Addiction medicine provides harm reduction for people who inject drugs, working alongside infectious disease and cardiothoracic surgery in the multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man who injects drugs has tricuspid valve endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus. He has no penicillin allergy. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Nafcillin or oxacillin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Penicillin G with gentamicin","Ceftriaxone with azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His cultures grow methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, and he has no penicillin allergy, so he is treated with nafcillin or oxacillin, or cefazolin. Susceptibility is the decisive clue, because vancomycin or daptomycin is the regimen for methicillin-resistant strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis has 2 days of worsening breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness when lying flat). A new, loud regurgitant murmur is heard and crackles fill both lung bases. Which mechanism explains her heart failure?","choices":["A toxic myocarditis from fever","Fluid overload from antibiotics","Constriction from the pericardium","Valvular dysfunction from infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction. Her new, loud regurgitant murmur reflects valvular damage or rupture of the chordae tendineae, and the resulting regurgitation explains her breathlessness, orthopnea and crackles at both lung bases. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve is having a tooth extracted tomorrow. He has no drug allergies and takes pills without difficulty. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Clindamycin before the visit","No prophylaxis is needed","Oral amoxicillin, 30-60 minutes before","Oral amoxicillin for 7 days after"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His prosthetic aortic valve is on the list of conditions for which prophylaxis is reasonable, and a tooth extraction manipulates the gums and roots of the teeth. With no allergy and no trouble swallowing, the regimen is a single dose of oral amoxicillin 2 g 30-60 minutes before the procedure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in urgent care has a fever, a new murmur and splinter hemorrhages (dark lines under the nail plates). Before transfer, a colleague proposes giving a dose of ceftriaxone right away. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Treating before cultures are drawn","Drawing three sets of cultures","Arranging hospital admission","Requesting an echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her fever, new murmur and splinter hemorrhages raise suspicion for endocarditis, and empiric therapy is started ONLY after blood cultures have been drawn, three sets from separate sites. Giving ceftriaxone first would compromise the cultures needed to identify the organism and direct the prolonged bactericidal course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors and a cough. A new holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and he looks toxic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Mitral valve endocarditis","Acute viral pericarditis","Tricuspid valve endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a person who injects drugs, fever, rigors and a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border point to tricuspid valve endocarditis, the valve named for this group (Staphylococcus aureus causes 80-90% of their tricuspid infections); the lung, a listed embolic site, explains the cough. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 11, 18"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and Janeway lesions (painless macules) on his palms. A transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the tricuspid valve. Blood cultures are still pending. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not classifiable yet","Definite endocarditis","Possible endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The oscillating mass on the tricuspid valve is a major criterion (positive echocardiogram), and he has three minor criteria: predisposition (injection drug use), fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and Janeway lesions as a vascular phenomenon. One major plus three minor criteria make endocarditis definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who injects drugs has native valve endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Ampicillin with gentamicin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Cefazolin alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus on a native valve, which is treated with vancomycin or daptomycin. Methicillin resistance is the decisive clue: it rules out nafcillin, oxacillin and cefazolin, which are used for susceptible strains. Therapy is bactericidal, parenteral and prolonged. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with a history of infective endocarditis has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), Janeway lesions (painless macules) on her soles and Osler nodes (painful nodules) on two finger pads. One of three blood cultures grows viridans streptococci, the other two are negative, and echocardiography is not yet available. How is her case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not classifiable yet","Definite endocarditis","Possible endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She has five minor criteria: predisposition (previous endocarditis), fever of 38.8 °C, a vascular phenomenon (Janeway lesions), an immunologic phenomenon (Osler nodes) and microbiologic evidence (one positive culture of three, which misses the major criterion). Five minor criteria make endocarditis definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman on long-term immunosuppression has endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow Candida. Which complication is most likely?","choices":["Embolic large artery occlusion","Rapid cure with medication","Isolated rheumatic carditis","Pericardial knock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her blood cultures grow Candida, which makes this fungal endocarditis, and the most likely complication of fungal endocarditis is embolism with occlusion of larger peripheral arteries. Her immunosuppression is the setting, but the organism is what predicts the complication. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
